  "findings": [
    "The database holds 230 validated flights across 3 flight dates (2026-10-09 to 2026-10-13); it is a snapshot of live data, not a time series.",
    "80.0% of flights are 'scheduled'; only 3 distinct status value(s) occur (scheduled, active, landed), so status carries little discriminating power.",
    "Only 125 of 230 flights (54.3%) report a delay value, so delay statistics describe that subset and not all flights.",
    "Among flights with delay data, 28 (22.4%) were delayed; the median delay is 0 min but the mean is 4.0 min and the maximum 68 min - the distribution is strongly right-skewed (skew 4.2).",
    "When a delay occurs it is typically 12 min (median of delayed flights); 4 delay(s) reach 30 min or more.",
    "Scheduled block time (timezone-corrected) has a median of 135 min and a maximum of 735 min; 17 flights (9.1%) lie above the IQR upper fence of 449 min. All are below the 24-hour validity limit, so they were retained and listed for review.",
    "Operated flights were delayed more often than codeshared listings (40% of 57 operated vs 7% of 68 codeshared; two-proportion z-test p < 0.001) - a statistically detectable difference. Codeshare rows are marketing listings of a partner's flight and delay values may not be reported for them in the same way, so this is an association in a small sample, not a cause.",
    "Scheduled departures peak at 19:00 local time (36 flights).",
    "101 airlines appear; the largest, Japan Airlines, accounts for 5.2% of flights - no single carrier dominates the sample.",
    "Live tracking data (speed, altitude, position) exists for only 16 flights (7.0%), so those fields support illustration rather than inference."
  ],
